.\" Copyright (C) 2023-2026 Kamila Szewczyk.  GPLv3-only.
.TH FASTENT 1 "May 2026" "fastent 1.3" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
fastent \- pseudorandom byte stream entropy and randomness tester

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fastent
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file | directory ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fastent
applies five tests of randomness to a byte stream and reports the
results: Shannon entropy, chi-square statistic and tail probability,
arithmetic mean, Monte Carlo value of \(*p, and serial correlation
coefficient.  The byte stream is read from
.I file
or, if no filename is given, from standard input.  Pass
.B \-r
to recurse into a
.IR directory ;
fastent then emits one row per regular file rather than a single
summary.
.PP
The inner loop is vectorised, uses a 4-way banked histogram, and
dispatches at runtime to the best available SIMD variant on the host
(scalar / SSSE3 / SSE4.1 / AVX2 / AVX-512 (+BITALG) / AArch64 NEON /
AArch64 SVE2 / ARMv7-A NEON / WebAssembly SIMD128).  Regular-file
inputs are memory-mapped or read via io_uring (Linux 5.1+) / IOCP
(Windows Vista+) and processed by an optional thread pool with
deterministic output.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bits
Treat input as a stream of bits rather than 8-bit bytes.  In bit mode
the histogram has two bins, the arithmetic mean is reported relative
to 0.5, and the chi-square statistic has one degree of freedom.
The Monte Carlo \(*p test remains byte-driven.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-counts
Print a per-value occurrence table.  In default output the table is
human-readable; with
.B \-t
it is rendered as CSV.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fold
Fold upper-case letters to their lower-case equivalents before
counting (ASCII A-Z and Latin-1 0xC0-0xDE excluding 0xD7).  The fold
is fused into the SIMD analyser body: each loaded vector is folded
in-register before the histogram, serial-correlation, and Monte
Carlo \(*p kernels consume it, so
.B \-f
keeps the full SIMD throughput rather than falling back to a scalar
prepass.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-terse
Emit results in CSV format suitable for downstream parsers.
.TP
.BR \-J ", " \-\-json
Emit results as a JSON document.  Overrides
.BR \-t .
With
.B \-c
the occurrences table is included as a nested array.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-extended
Also report the extended statistics: min-entropy
.RB ( H \(if ),
collision entropy
.RB ( H 2)
and index of coincidence, the poker test (16-bin nibble chi-square,
15 degrees of freedom), variance and standard deviation, redundancy,
the distinct-symbol count, the most/least common symbol, and the
per-bit-position bias (the probability that bit
.IR k ,
0 = LSB .. 7 = MSB, is set; byte mode only).  These are appended to
the default, terse, JSON, and recursive output; without
.B \-e
the output is byte-for-byte the classic format.  All extended
quantities are derived at finalisation, so they add no per-byte cost
and remain bit-identical across hosts and thread counts.
.IP
.B \-e
is repeatable.  Passing it twice
.RB ( \-ee ", " "or " \-e\~\-e ", or " \-\-extended\~\-\-extended )
additionally computes the order-1 bigram conditional entropy
.RB H( cur | prev ),
the adjacent mutual information
.RB I( prev ; cur ),
a longest identical-symbol run, a runs test, and a bit-mode cusum
max excursion.  The order-0 statistics keep full SIMD throughput; the
level-2 reductions are split across the
.B \-j
workers (per-slab on mmap, an SPMC pipeline on stream and io_uring)
and merged with a boundary stitch, deterministic and bit-identical
across thread counts.  Byte mode keeps two 64K u64 tables per worker
thread (1 MiB); bit mode is free.
.IP
Passing it three times
.RB ( \-eee ", or " \-\-extended " thrice)"
additionally runs the
.B LZ77F
estimator: a count-only LZ77F match finder (acceleration 1,
HLOG 13) that never emits a byte.  It reports the compressibility
excess over an incompressible baseline
.RB ( lz_cr_excess ),
the literal byte-value Shannon entropy and its KL divergence to
uniform
.RB ( lz_lit_h " / " lz_lit_kl ),
the match coverage
.RB ( lz_match_cov ),
the offset and match-length concentration
.RB ( lz_off_conc " / " lz_mlen_excess ;
each takes its continuous 0 limit below two matches), an advisory
literal chi-square against uniform
.RB ( lz_lit_chi ,
255 degrees of freedom, kept separate from the headline as order-0
entropy and its chi-square are kept separate), and a headline
.B lz_deviation
z =
.RB max( S1 ", " S2 /8 ", " S3 )
over
.RB sigma0 " = max(2/" n ", 2" \[u207B]\[u2077] ),
badged
.BR PASS / WEAK / FAIL
(z < 2 / z < 3 / z >= 3).  A pristine periodic or exact-duplicate
input that collapses to a single dominant match (fewer than two
matches with high coverage) is flagged explicitly so a zero
offset/length concentration does not read as no structure; the
verdict still fails via the compressibility and coverage signals.
The parse is keyed to a fixed 4 MiB block grid on the absolute
file offset with a fresh hash table per block, so the count-only
size and every table are exactly additive: the result is
bit-identical for any thread count, for
.BR \-\-io=mmap ", " stream " or " uring ,
and across hosts.  Drift versus a whole-file table-carrying serial
parse is at most about 0.055 percent and verdict-neutral.  Reported
in the default, terse, JSON, recursive and annotated output.
.IP
.B \-eee
additionally runs a windowed linear-complexity estimator alongside
LZ77F.  Each whole 512-bit (64-byte) window of the stream is reduced
by GF(2) Berlekamp-Massey to its linear complexity
.IR L ,
the length of the shortest LFSR that generates it.  It reports the
mean
.I L
.RB ( bm_mean_lc ),
the random-sequence expectation
.RB ( bm_mu ),
a headline
.B bm_deviation
z =
.RB | bm_mean_lc \- bm_mu |
over
.RB sqrt( VarL / W )
badged
.BR PASS / WEAK / FAIL
(z < 2 / z < 3 / z >= 3), and an advisory NIST SP800-22
linear-complexity class chi-square
.RB ( bm_chi ,
5 degrees of freedom: the two extreme tail classes are pooled).
A near-constant stream (mean
.I L
below 2) is flagged like the LZ77F single-dominant-match note; the
verdict still fails via the large z.  Bits are read most-significant
first per byte, the same convention as
.BR \-b "."
64 divides the 4 MiB grid, so a window never straddles a block and
the result is bit-identical for any thread count, I/O mode and host
with zero drift.  Scope: GF(2) Berlekamp-Massey catches LFSR-class
and low-bit linear recurrences (a maximal 16-bit LFSR scores
.I L
near 16, far below mu = 256) but not a truncated-high-byte LCG,
whose output bits carry no short GF(2) recurrence.
.IP
.B \-eee
also runs a windowed Maurer universal statistical test alongside
LZ77F and the linear-complexity estimator.  The bit stream is read
most-significant first per byte and cut into
.I L
= 8-bit blocks; after
.I Q
= 10*2^L initialisation blocks, each further block adds the base-2
logarithm of the distance back to the previous occurrence of its
pattern.  It reports the statistic
.RB ( maurer_fn ,
the mean of those logarithms), the NIST SP800-22 expected value for
.I L
= 8
.RB ( maurer_expected ),
and a headline
.B maurer_deviation
z =
.RB | maurer_fn \- maurer_expected |
over
.RB ( c "*" sqrt( variance / K ))
badged
.BR PASS / WEAK / FAIL
(z < 2 / z < 3 / z >= 3).  A highly repetitive stream (fn far below
expected) is flagged; the verdict still fails via the large z.
.I L
is fixed at 8 so a 4 MiB window holds about 4.19 million test
blocks, sixteen times the NIST
.I K
>= 1000*2^L validity floor (L = 16 would fall about forty-five
times short).  Each 4 MiB grid block uses a fresh recency table and
the per-block partial sums combine in absolute block index order,
so the result is bit-identical for any thread count, I/O mode and
host; drift versus a textbook whole-stream table-carrying Maurer is
bounded and verdict-neutral (at most about 0.001%, 0.00073%
measured on a 100 MB English text corpus).  Scope: Maurer is a
global compressibility statistic ("how compressible under an
idealised universal compressor"); it catches repetitive or
compressible sources that pass order-0/-1, LZ77F and the
linear-complexity screen but does not locate the structure and does
not replace the per-test advisories.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-annotate
Render an interpretive report instead of the plain text: every metric
is shown with a
.BR PASS / WEAK / FAIL
verdict and a one-line explanation, followed by a headline naming the
weakest signal.  Implies
.BR \-e .
Not supported with
.BR \-r ;
.BR \-\-json " and " \-t
take precedence if combined.  Verdict colour follows
.BR \-\-color .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-full-precision
Render every floating-point value at \fB%.17g\fR (machine precision).
.TP
.BR \-j " " \fIN\fR ", " \-\-threads= \fIN\fR
Use
.I N
worker threads (default 1).  Pass
.B "\-j auto"
to use one thread per online CPU
.RB ( sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN) ).
Threading covers every input path: mmap slices the resident buffer
into per-thread slabs, while stdin/stream and io_uring use an SPMC
pipeline (one serialized read at a time, analysis in parallel).
Blocks are aligned to multiples of six so the Monte Carlo \(*p state
machine never crosses threads, and the boundary products, digram
pair, longest run, bit runs and cusum walk are stitched in stream
order.  Output is byte-for-byte identical to the single-threaded run
because all arithmetic is integer and the merge order is
deterministic.
This option is present only when
.B fastent
was built with a thread backend; a single-threaded build omits both
.B \-j
and
.B \-\-threads
(and rejects them as unknown).
.TP
.BR \-i ", " "\-\-io=" \fIMODE\fR
Pick the input strategy.  \fIMODE\fR is one of:
.RS
.IP \fBauto\fR 8
Default.  Memory-map regular files; fall back to streaming reads
for stdin, pipes, and unmappable sources.
.IP \fBmmap\fR 8
Memory-mapped only; error out if the input cannot be mapped.
.IP \fBstream\fR 8
2 MiB
.BR read (2)
loop in all cases.
.IP \fBuring\fR 8
Asynchronous, four-way pipelined reads.  io_uring on Linux 5.1+
(raw
.BR io_uring_setup "(2) / " io_uring_enter (2),
no liburing dependency); IOCP on Windows Vista+ (overlapped
.BR ReadFile " + " GetQueuedCompletionStatus ).
ENOSYS on other targets.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-histogram
Render a vertical block-plot of the byte (or bit) distribution
before the textual results.  Uses Unicode 1/8-th block glyphs
(U+2581..U+2588) on terminals that support UTF-8, single-byte CP 437
glyphs on DOS / Win9x consoles, and ASCII '#' on stdout when
redirected.  A Y-axis scale labelled with the largest bin count and
an X-axis tick row aligned under the bars frame the plot.  The
histogram and other output formats compose: the
histogram is rendered before the human-readable text but is also
included alongside
.BR \-t " and " \-\-json
when those are set.
.IP
Under
.BR \-eee ,
five extra plots follow the byte/bit plot, using the same
Unicode-glyph,
.BR \-\-log ,
Y-axis-scale and X-tick rendering: the LZ77F match offsets
(1..65535) and match lengths (4..255+) in log2 buckets, the
LZ77F literal byte-value histogram (256 bins, its title carrying
H_lit), the linear-complexity
.I L
histogram (64 bins over 0..512), and the Maurer log2-distance
histogram (64 bins).  They compose with
.BR \-t " and " \-\-json
exactly as the byte/bit plot does.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log
Logarithmic Y axis for the histogram.  Useful when one bin dominates.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " "\-\-color=" \fIMODE\fR
Colour mode for the histogram.  \fIMODE\fR is
.BR auto " (default; colour iff stdout is a TTY and " NO_COLOR " unset), "
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
Bytes in [0,32) and 127 render in dim grey (control chars), [32,128)
in default foreground (ASCII printable), [128,256) in accent
(high-bit / Latin-1).  Colour is emitted as ANSI escapes on POSIX,
.BR SetConsoleTextAttribute (3)
on Windows (works on Win95+ without ANSI.SYS), and DJGPP
.IR <conio.h>
.BR textcolor / cputs
BIOS-attribute writes on MS-DOS.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Treat the positional argument as a directory; walk it recursively
and emit one row per regular file.  Output format is CSV by default
or JSON under
.BR \-\-json ;
the
.B path
column comes first, followed by the same six summary metrics as
.BR \-t ,
and the extended columns when
.B \-e
is in effect.
Walks do not cross symlinks (except at the root).  Unreadable files
or directories print a stderr warning and are skipped.
.TP
.B "\-\-sort-by=" \fICOL\fR [: \fIDIR\fR ]
Sort the rows in recursive mode.
.I COL
is one of
.BR path ", " samples ", " entropy ", " chisq ", " mean ", " pi ", " scc ", "
.BR min-entropy ", " collision ", " ic ", " poker ", " variance ", "
.BR redundancy ", " distinct ", " bitbias ", "
.BR cond-entropy ", " mutual-info ", "
.BR lz-deviation ", " lz-cr ", " lz-match-cov ", "
.BR bm-deviation ", " bm-mean-lc ", " maurer-deviation .
Selecting any of the extended columns implies
.BR \-e ;
the
.B cond-entropy
and
.B mutual-info
columns imply
.BR \-ee ;
the
.BR lz-deviation ", " lz-cr ", " lz-match-cov ", " bm-deviation
" and " bm-mean-lc
columns imply
.BR \-eee .
.I DIR
is
.B asc
or
.BR desc ;
default is descending for the numeric columns and ascending for
.BR path .
.TP
.B \-\-fips\-140\-2
Run the FIPS 140-2 RNG power-up self-tests (4.9.1) instead of the
normal analysis: monobit, poker, runs and long run, applied
independently to each 20000-bit (2500-byte) block.  Bits are taken
most-significant first; the poker groups are the high then the low
nibble of each byte.  The acceptance constants are the FIPS 140-2
values (monobit 9725 < ones < 10275; poker 2.16 < X < 46.17; the runs
intervals; long run a run of 34 or more; the rng-tools
.B rngtest
utility historically uses 26).  Trailing bytes that do not fill a
block are not tested.  A pass/fail report is printed and the exit
status is 0 if every block passed all four tests, 1 otherwise; other
output options are ignored.  The independent blocks are split across
the
.B \-j
workers and the result is bit-identical regardless of thread count.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the program version and exit.

.SH "OUTPUT FORMAT"
By default,
.B fastent
emits six paragraphs of human-readable text reporting each of the
five tests plus an estimate of optimum byte- or bit-wise
compressibility.
.PP
In terse mode (\fB\-t\fR), two lines of CSV are written:

.RS
.nf
0,File-bytes,Entropy,Chi-square,P-Exceed,Mean,Monte-Carlo-Pi,Serial-Correlation
1,<samples>,<entropy>,<chi-square>,<p-exceed>,<mean>,<monte-pi>,<scc>
.fi
.RE

In bit mode the first field is
.B File-bits
instead of
.BR File-bytes .
With
.BR \-c ,
two additional sections appear:

.RS
.nf
2,Value,Occurrences,Fraction
3,<value>,<count>,<fraction>
\&...
.fi
.RE

In JSON mode (\fB\-\-json\fR), the structure is:

.RS
.nf
{
  "unit": "byte",
  "samples": <integer>,
  "entropy": <float>,
  "optimum_compression_percent": <integer>,
  "chi_square": { "statistic": <float>, "df": <int>, "p_exceed": <float> },
  "arithmetic_mean": <float>,
  "monte_carlo_pi": { "value": <float>, "error_percent": <float> },
  "serial_correlation": <float | null>,
  "occurrences": [ {"value":<int>,"count":<int>,"fraction":<float>}, ... ]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B serial_correlation
field is
.B null
when the input contains only a single distinct value (the
human-readable output renders this case as
\fIundefined (all values equal!)\fR).
.PP
Under
.B \-r
the CSV header becomes
.RS
.nf
path,unit,samples,entropy,chi_square,p_exceed,mean,\
monte_carlo_pi,serial_correlation
.fi
.RE
with one data line per file (paths are RFC 4180-quoted when they
contain a comma, double quote, or newline).  JSON recursive output
nests the file rows under
.B files
in a top-level object:
.RS
.nf
{
  "unit": "byte",
  "files": [
    { "path": "...", "samples": <int>, "entropy": <float>, ... },
    ...
  ]
}
.fi
.RE
Any numeric field whose value would be NaN (e.g.\&
.B monte_carlo_pi
on a sub-hexad-sized file) is written as JSON
.BR null .
.PP
Under
.B \-e
the extended statistics are appended to every format.  The terse and
recursive CSV rows gain, in order,
.BR Min-Entropy ", " Collision-Entropy ", " IC ", " Poker ", " Poker-p ", "
.BR Variance ", " Stddev ", " Redundancy ", " Distinct ", " Mode ", "
.BR Mode-Count ", " Rarest ", " Rarest-Count ", "
.BR Bit0 .. Bit7 ", " Bit-Bias-Max ", " Bit-Bias-Worst ", "
.BR Conditional-Entropy ", " Mutual-Information ", "
.BR Runs ", " Longest-Run ", " Cusum-Max .
The JSON object gains
.BR min_entropy ", " collision_entropy ", " index_of_coincidence ", "
a
.B poker
sub-object
.RB ( null
in bit mode),
.BR variance ", " stddev ", " redundancy ", " distinct_symbols ", "
and the
.BR most_common " and " rarest
sub-objects, the
.B bit_frequencies
array (8 values, or
.B null
in bit mode) and the
.B bit_bias
sub-object
.RB ( max ", " worst_bit ).
With
.B \-ee
the
.BR conditional_entropy ", " mutual_information ", " runs ", "
.BR longest_run " and " cusum_max
fields are populated (otherwise
.BR null ).
In
.BR \-\-annotate
mode the plain text is replaced by an aligned table: one row per
metric with its value, p-value where defined, a
.BR PASS / WEAK / FAIL / "N/A"
badge, and a short interpretation, closing with a
.B VERDICT
headline that names the weakest signal.  Entropy-ratio verdicts are
advisory; the chi-square, poker, mean, and serial-correlation verdicts
are derived from the p-value or a z-score against the uniform
sampling distribution.

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B fastent
returns 0 on success, 1 on argument parsing failure, and 2 if the
input file cannot be opened.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Test the entropy of 1 MiB of kernel randomness:
.IP
.B head -c 1M /dev/urandom | fastent
.PP
Bit-level analysis of a JPEG, with the histogram included:
.IP
.B fastent -bc photo.jpg
.PP
Machine-readable summary of a tarball:
.IP
.B fastent --json large.tar
.PP
Full-precision terse output for a benchmark:
.IP
.B fastent -tp random.bin
.PP
Walk a directory and sort files by entropy, descending:
.IP
.B fastent -r --sort-by=entropy /var/log
.PP
Same as JSON, including the per-file path column, on cold-cache
NVMe data:
.IP
.B fastent -r --json --io=uring /mnt/data > entropy.json
.PP
Visualise the byte distribution of a tarball with a log Y axis:
.IP
.B fastent -H --log archive.tar.gz

.SH PERFORMANCE
.B fastent
reads input via memory mapping with
.BR madvise (2)
.B MADV_SEQUENTIAL
and
.BR posix_fadvise (2)
.B POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL
when the input is a regular file.  On Linux 5.1+ and Windows Vista+
.B \-\-io=uring
trades that for a four-deep async pipeline (io_uring on Linux, IOCP
on Win32) which hides latency on cold-cache NVMe;  hot-cache
throughput is unchanged.  Otherwise input goes through a 2 MiB
.BR read (2)
loop.
.PP
The histogram inner loop uses four banked sub-histograms to break
the read-modify-write hazard chain; the serial-correlation
cross-product is computed via signed widened multiply + horizontal
add (the SSE / AVX2 path with a sign-correction term derived from
the byte sum, the AVX-512 path with the same algorithm widened to
64-byte vectors).  Bit-mode population count uses
.B VPOPCNTB
on AVX-512 BITALG hosts and a PSHUFB nibble-LUT fallback otherwise.
The AArch64 SVE2 path uses
.B svdot_u32
for the cross-product and runs at the host's native vector length
(128 to 2048 bits per SVE2 spec).
.PP
On a recent x86_64 host with AVX-512 single-thread throughput sits
around 2.5 GB/s (byte) / 4.7 GB/s (bit); a saturated
.B \-j auto
sweep reaches roughly 15-17 GB/s before DDR4 bandwidth caps it.  See
the README for the per-host scaling plot.

.SH PLATFORMS
.B fastent
is portable across every Unix-like host with a working C99 compiler
and libc.  Pre-built binaries are published for each release, all
fully static (except macOS, which links the platform SDK stub):
.IP \(bu 2
Linux on x86_64, i386, aarch64, armhf, riscv64, ppc64le, ppc64,
powerpc, s390x, loongarch64, mips, mipsel, mips64, mips64el, alpha,
sparc64, sparc, m68k, hppa, arc, sh4.
.IP \(bu 2
Windows on x86_64, i686, and aarch64; a separate Win95-compatible
i686 build limits its import table to
.B kernel32.dll
and
.B msvcrt.dll
and runs on every Windows release from 95 onward.
.IP \(bu 2
macOS on x86_64 and aarch64.
.IP \(bu 2
MS-DOS on i386 (DJGPP + CWSDPMI baked in; no separate DPMI host
needed).
WebAssembly on wasm32 and wasm64 (emscripten, self-contained
single-file
.B node
launcher with NODERAWFS host-fs bridge); both run as
.B "node fastent.js" .
The wasm64 launcher needs node 24 or later (memory64 stabilised in
V8 13).
.PP
SIMD fast paths cover x86 (SSSE3 / SSE4.1 / AVX2 / AVX-512 with
optional BITALG), AArch64 + ARMv7-A (NEON), AArch64 (SVE2), and
WebAssembly (SIMD128).  Every other target falls back to the scalar
loop, which is still 4-way banked and keeps most of the per-byte
throughput.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B fastent
does not consult any environment variables.  The thread count comes
from
.B \-j
/
.B \-\-threads
on the command line only.

.SH "DETERMINISM"
Output is byte-for-byte reproducible across thread counts.  The
internal arithmetic is all integer, slab boundaries are aligned to
multiples of six so the Monte Carlo \(*p state machine never crosses
a thread, and the merge order is fixed.  A given input produces
the same numbers under
.B \-j 1
and
.BR "\-j auto" .

.SH "MEMORY USE"
Regular-file inputs are memory-mapped read-only: virtual address
space proportional to the input, resident-set growth driven by the
kernel's read-ahead.  Stream inputs (stdin, pipes, unmappable files,
.BR \-\-io=stream )
use a single 2 MiB aligned read buffer regardless of input size.
Each worker thread under
.B \-j N
holds a small per-slab accumulator (a few KiB) plus the histogram
bins; total resident overhead under
.B "\-j auto"
stays well under a megabyte on top of the mapped region.  The one
exception is
.B \-ee
in byte mode, which adds two 64K u64 digram tables (1 MiB) per
worker thread plus one aggregate; bit-mode
.B \-ee
adds only a 2-by-2 table.

.SH BUGS
The optimum-compression figure derives from the first-order
(byte-marginal or bit-marginal) Shannon entropy and is therefore a
lower bound: it ignores any structure beyond a single byte/bit,
including correlations, repeats, and longer-range patterns that a
real compressor (e.g.\&
.BR xz (1),
.BR zstd (1))
would exploit.  A source whose byte histogram is uniform but whose
sample sequence is highly structured (digits of \(*p, an LCG stream)
is reported as 0% compressible despite being reproducible from a
tiny seed.
.PP
A perfectly tight bound would require the Kolmogorov complexity of
the input -- the length of the shortest program that emits it.
The author gladly offers co-authorship on the resulting paper
to anyone who computes that quantity efficiently for arbitrary
inputs.

